.TH AMR 2
.SH NAME
amr: new, adapt, exchange, step, sweep, refine, coarsen, get, set, clear, splat, totalmass \- block-structured adaptive mesh refinement
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "amr.m";
amr := load Amr Amr->PATH;
Forest, Block: import amr;

f := amr->new(nx0, ny0, nz0, blocksize, maxlevel, domainx, domainy, domainz);
amr->adapt(f, crit);
amr->sweep(f, diffusivity, dt);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Amr
is block-structured adaptive mesh refinement, the shape miniAMR itself
uses: the domain is tiled at level 0 by an
.IR nx0 " x " ny0 " x " nz0
grid of
.BR Block s,
each holding
.IR blocksize \(ha3
cells. Refining a block replaces it with 8 level+1 children, each
still
.IR blocksize \(ha3
cells but covering half the parent's physical extent per axis \(em
refinement halves the cell size, not the block's own cell count. This
is the real distinction from an arbitrary octree of individual cells:
whole blocks refine or coarsen as a unit.
.PP
.B New
creates a forest with the given root grid, block size, refinement cap,
and physical domain size.
.B Cellsize
gives the per-cell physical size at a level;
.B Blockbox
gives a block's physical centre and half-extent \(em what a
.B Criterion
is evaluated against.
.B Activeblocks
lists every leaf (active) block, in a stable order.
.PP
.B Refine
splits a block into 8 finer children, seeding each with a real
piecewise-constant injection of the parent's own values.
.B Coarsen
merges a refined block's 8 children back via real restriction (each
interior cell becomes the volume-average of the matching 2x2x2 block
of fine cells); it is a no-op unless all 8 children are themselves
leaves \(em coarsening only ever removes one level at a time, bottom-up.
Refining then immediately coarsening the same block is an exact
identity on the data, for any field, not only a smooth one: injection
replicates each coarse cell into its matching 8 fine cells, and
restriction exactly averages those replicas back.
.SS "Adaptation and 2:1 balance"
.B Criterion
is a bare function reference (Limbo has no closure literals \(em the
same convention as
.IR krylov (2)'s
.B Apply
/
.IR fem (2)'s
.BR Src ),
given a block's physical centre and half-extent, returning whether it
should be refined.
.PP
.B Adapt
runs one full adaptation pass: refines every active block
.I crit
flags (down to
.IR maxlevel ),
first extending the refine set until the forest is 2:1 balanced \(em
no two face-adjacent leaves differ by more than one level, the
standard block-AMR invariant that bounds
.BR exchange ()
to only ever needing a same-level, one-level-coarser, or up-to-4
one-level-finer neighbour per face, not an arbitrary jump. It then
coarsens every refined block whose own box
.I crit
no longer flags, whose children are all themselves leaves,
.B and
whose own level is still 2:1-balanced against its neighbours after
coarsening \(em skipping that last check would silently undo any
refinement that balancing alone required (crit() never wants such a
block back, so it would be coarsened again on the very next call).
.SS "Ghost exchange and the stencil sweep"
.B Sweep
is now simply
.BR step .
.B Step
exchanges at the top of every substep, including the first, so a
.B sweep
that exchanged before calling it was doing a whole extra exchange of
every block \(em the entire cost of one, when the step took a single
substep.
.PP
.B Step
computes through
.IR math (2)'s
.BR lap7 ,
for which a block is already exactly the padded cube it wants, and takes
each block's new array rather than copying it back cell by cell.
Nothing reads another block's data during the stencil loop \(em it
touches only a block's own interior and its own halo \(em so there is no
ordering to preserve.
Five sweeps of a 4\(mu4\(mu4 forest went from 199ms to 44ms at
blocksize 8, and from 4817ms to 603ms at blocksize 16, with the mass
identical to the bit.
.PP
.B Exchange
fills every active block's 1-cell ghost halo from its neighbours:
same-level neighbour \(em direct copy; one-level-coarser neighbour \(em
piecewise-constant prolongation of its boundary cells; one-level-finer
neighbours (up to 4 per face) \(em volume-averaged restriction of
their boundary cells. At the domain's own outer boundary, ghosts are
filled by clamping (zero-Neumann), matching
.IR pde (2)'s
.BR CLAMP .
.PP
.B Step
runs one explicit 7-point 3D Laplacian diffusion update on every
active block's interior cells, using each block's own (already-
exchanged) ghost halo, internally subdividing
.I dt
to satisfy a stability limit sized off the
.B finest
active level \(em the same conservative, always-safe-for-coarser-
blocks convention
.IR pde (2)'s
own
.B diffuse
uses, generalised across mixed resolutions.
.B Sweep
is
.B exchange
then
.BR step ,
what a driver loop calls each iteration.
.PP
On a forest with no refinement at all,
.B sweep
reproduces
.IR pde (2)'s
own
.B diffuse
exactly (verified to float-noise precision against an equivalent
.IR pde (2)
field run side by side with the same initial condition and step).
.SS "Field access"
.BR Get " and " set
read or write one cell by its block-local
.RI ( x , y , z )
index (there is no single global index \(em AMR has none).
.B Clear
sets every active cell to a value;
.B splat
sets every active cell within a physical radius of a point.
.B Totalmass
sums
.IR value \(mu cellsize\(ha3
over every active cell \(em the invariant a diffusion sweep should
conserve on a single-resolution forest (verified); a mixed-resolution
forest is not guaranteed to conserve it exactly, since this
implementation's ghost exchange does not perform a flux-matching
correction at coarse-fine boundaries \(em a real, documented scope
limit, not an oversight.
.PP
.IR Amrtest (1)
measures all three cases rather than leaving the reader to guess how
much that limit costs.
A uniform forest, at level 0 or with every block refined, conserves mass
to about
.BR 1e\-14 ,
which is to say exactly.
A forest adapted around a ball, 608 leaves with real coarse-fine
interfaces, loses about
.B 5%
of its mass over twenty sweeps.
That is the scope limit, not a defect, but it is a large enough number
to know before relying on it.
